{"name":"Glass Tile","hex":"#cdd0c0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"glass","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An amorphous solid, often transparent substance, usually made by melting silica sand with various additives (for most purposes, a mixture of soda, potash and lime is added).","Any amorphous solid (one without a regular crystal lattice)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To fit with glass; to glaze.","To enclose in glass."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glass"},{"term":"tile","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A regularly-shaped slab of clay or other material, affixed to cover or decorate a surface, as in a roof-tile, glazed tile, stove tile, carpet tile, etc.","A rectangular graphic."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover with tiles.","To arrange in a regular pattern, with adjoining edges (applied to tile-like objects, graphics, windows in a computer interface)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tile"}],"entries":[{"title":"Glass tile","blocks":[{"text":"Glass tiles are pieces of glass formed into consistent shapes, used as a building material and decorative surface. They are produced by methods ranging from traditional opaque smalti casting to modern fused-glass and digitally printed processes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Hakatai_glass_tile_2.jpg/330px-Hakatai_glass_tile_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glass_tile","wikidata":"Q5567135"},{"title":"Glass","blocks":[{"text":"Glass is an amorphous (non-crystalline) solid. Because it is often transparent and chemically inert, glass has found widespread practical, technological, and decorative use in window panes, tableware, and optics. Some common objects made of glass are named after the material, e.g., a \"glass\" for drinking, \"glasses\" for vision correction, and a \"magnifying glass\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Fassade_Wilhelmstrasse_65%2C_Berlin-Mitte%2C_160417%2C_ako.jpg/330px-Fassade_Wilhelmstrasse_65%2C_Berlin-Mitte%2C_160417%2C_ako.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glass","wikidata":"Q11469"},{"title":"Tile","blocks":[{"text":"Tiles are usually thin, square rectangular coverings manufactured from hard-wearing material such as ceramic, stone, metal, baked clay, or even glass. They are generally fixed in place in an array to cover roofs, floors, walls, edges, or other objects such as tabletops. Alternatively, tile can sometimes refer to similar units made from lightweight materials such as perlite, wood, and mineral wool, typically used for wall and ceiling applications. In another sense, a tile is a construction tile or similar object, such as rectangular counters used in playing games. The word is derived from the French word tuile, which is, in turn, from the Latin word tegula, meaning a roof tile composed of fired clay, otherwise known as nga."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Alban_chambon%2C_decorazione_in_marmo_e_ceramica%2C_dal_castello_reale_delle_ardenne%2C_houyet%2C_1905_ca._02.jpg/330px-Alban_chambon%2C_decorazione_in_marmo_e_ceramica%2C_dal_castello_reale_delle_ardenne%2C_houyet%2C_1905_ca._02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tile","wikidata":"Q468402"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/GlassTileHome_Crystal_Glass.jpg/500px-GlassTileHome_Crystal_Glass.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/GlassTileHome_Crystal_Glass.jpg/500px-GlassTileHome_Crystal_Glass.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Closeup of glass tile sticks on a mesh tile square.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGlassTileHome_Crystal_Glass.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Platform_Diving%2C_1994.jpg/500px-Platform_Diving%2C_1994.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Platform_Diving%2C_1994.jpg/500px-Platform_Diving%2C_1994.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Glass mosaics of sea turtles on a subway platform","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APlatform_Diving%2C_1994.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Lipari-Obsidienne_%285%29.jpg/250px-Lipari-Obsidienne_%285%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Lipari-Obsidienne_%285%29.jpg/500px-Lipari-Obsidienne_%285%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Lipari-Obsidienne_%285%29.jpg/250px-Lipari-Obsidienne_%285%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALipari-Obsidienne_(5).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Moldavite_Besednice.jpg/330px-Moldavite_Besednice.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Moldavite_Besednice.jpg/960px-Moldavite_Besednice.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Moldavite_Besednice.jpg/330px-Moldavite_Besednice.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMoldavite_Besednice.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.o